<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="explaining-reasoning" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Explaining Your Reasoning in Written Work</title>
    <p>
       Explaining your reasoning clearly is a key part of the homework process. 
       One of the course learning goals is <q>communication dexterity</q>. 
       This is an important skill by itself, since most of modern science and engineering is 
       collaborative and requires careful explanations of your process to other people.
    </p>
    <p>
     Clear communication is also good as a study tool: you will likely forget some of what you
     have done when we move to new content, so you should write your notes in such a way 
     that you can reference them later when you are studying. <q>Later</q>, by the way, might be years down 
     the line when you see a problem again in a different class! Having good documentation can save future you from a lot of frustration.
    </p>
    <p>
    If you do not explain problems clearly, one possible consequence is we may ask you to resubmit one or more problems. 
    We understand this can be frustrating, but the goal is to help you. When working on complex problems, 
    having the ability to approach them with a high standard of logic and clarity will help you not to get lost. 
    Your solution does not have to be an essay. But notations should be defined (a picture is sufficient); 
    equations should be labeled (what they represent), and important choices need to be specified.
    </p>
    <p> 
        Q: How do I know what choice is important enough to be explained? I can't read your mind! This seems so arbitrary and unfair!
    </p>
    <p>
        A: Understanding what the problem is about (and what needs to be explained) is part of understanding the material. 
        Imagine you're hiring a contractor to build a wooden structure in your back yard and you ask them, <q>tell me how you'll make it structurally sound</q>.
        But instead of telling you what structure of beams and supports they're planning (which is what you're asking), 
        they first start telling you at length how theylll make sure any planks are hammered together with nails, as it's really-really important; 
        and they'll also make sure the wood is solid and not rotten<ellipsis/> 
        Both are valid points, yet you'll immediately conclude they barely know what they're talking about! 
    </p>
    <p>
        Parable out of the way, here are some things that should always be explained in your written work:
    </p>
    <p>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Defining Variables</title>
                <p>
                    What your variables mean, as well as distinguishing between scalar and vector variables. 
                    This will almost always require some sort of coordinate system or reference to be specified.
                    It will <alert>certainly require that you draw a picture for every problem.</alert>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Plan of Attack</title>
                
                <p>
                  You should outline your plan of attack for a given problem. This can go together with the math,
                  but before your write down an equation, you should explain <em>why you are writing it
                  in the first place</em>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Specify Systems Clearly</title>
                <p>
                    You should be very specific as to which physical principles you are applying to which systems
                    (ex: <q>using Newton's second law for the heavy block</q>, <q>mechanical energy remains constant for
                    the pair of masses and the Earth during this specific process</q>). As discussed in class, quantities like
                    momentum and energy are system-dependent, so without a clearly defined system, whatever equation follows
                    is not meaningful.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Sanity Checks</title>
                <p>
                    Most problems should have a sanity check. To sanity check your answer, you should start by trying to make a guess for
                    what the answer should be before you solve the problem. You don't have to write this down, and it's OK if you're wrong. 
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </p>
    <p>
        Based on previous work, here are some notes to help you understand why I want you
        to do this, which will reference specific mistakes I've observed in some student work.
        Note: if you made this mistake, please don't think I am trying to judge you. The goal
        here is to show how adding a small amount of structure can help you avoid mistakes
        in the future. As the saying goes, <q>an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure</q>
    </p>
    <p>
        Notes on variables, diagrams, and coordinates:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If a variable shows up in your mathematical solution, it needs to either be defined in the problem statement 
                    *explicitly* or defined in a diagram. If you're using an angle <m>\alpha</m>: angle with which direction? 
                    If you're using a distance: distance from what? If you're talking about a displacement or velocity, what is the origin? 
                    What are the + and – directions? Most mistakes on problems 2 and 3 in the midterm go back to this in one way or another.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Using coordinate systems does *not* just mean <q>slap x and y axes in a figure</q> and be done. 
                    A good number of students got confused, e.g., in problem 3 of the midterm, because they had a vertical 
                    axes in their picture but the distance downwards from the pulley to to the mass clearly increases downwards,
                    and if that is your <q>y-direction</q>, this affects how you write the 2nd law equations.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Clearly distinguish between vector variables <m>\vec{u}</m> and their components <m>~{u_x,u_y}</m>, and magnitude <m>u</m>. 
                    If a vector points directly along an axis (e.g x), remember that its component will be <m>\~{pm u}</m> (depending on whether
                    the vector points in the +x or -x direction). Many mistakes on problem 1 of the midterm 
                    for not noticing that you were implicitly defining the <term>velocity</term> of the current relative to the ground as
                    <m>~{\vec{u}=-u\hat{i}}</m> and clearly distinguishing between the vector, its x component, and the magnitude.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
        Notes on plan of attack:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    This is what real experts do when solving problems. No one starts with equations or computations, you start by
                    identifying which conceptual and mathematical tools are relevant. That's part of why I want you to practice
                    this on your work.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The other part is that the feedback process is much better when you write down your ideas. That way the graders
                    can tell you more than <q>the equation is wrong here</q>, but they can help you study by evaluating your ideas.
                    This is the level of feedback you should <alert>demand</alert> from your homework.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Many of the individual terms that go in an equation come from a diagram, so the best way to make this clear
                    is by having a good diagram in the first place. Statements that you should be writing often might look something
                    like <q>We want to find the acceleration, so let's use FBD + pictures to write down Newton's 2nd law for each of the masses</q>;
                    <q>We can find the change in momentum for system X by using our before-after-picture, and we know the external forces from the FBD”</q>;
                    <q>I'm swimming directly North, which means that my velocity relative to the ground points North, and this relates to the
                    current velocity in the way shown in the picture</q>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    You do not ever have to explain algebra and arithmetic in great detail, but I have noticed lots of mistakes from trying to write
                    one equation and simplify it all in one go in your head without writing intermediate steps. This is generally not a good idea, take
                    it easy.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
        Notes on sanity checks:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Start easy: you solved the problem and found X = (expression), does (expression) have the same units as X?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Check for basic mathematical consistency: if your expression has a square root, 
                    are you sure that the thing inside of it is positive? 
                    Does your expression have the correct sign? 
                    Do note that this will sometimes require having a good understanding of vectors vs magnitudes.
                    A good chunk of mistakes in problem 3 of the midterm could have been caught by noticing a denominator
                    like <m>m-M</m>, which would lead to an unreasonable infinity.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Quick addendum to the previous note: sometimes the possibility of an infinity or a negative square
                    root isn't wrong, but indicates that certain constraints need to be satisfied. That can be useful
                    to note, as well
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Look for limiting cases that are simple. This could be something like setting an angle to be 0 or
                    90 degrees, or a mass to zero or infinity. The goal of doing this is to transform the general problem
                    into one you can guess the solution in your head (mostly) <em>before</em> you do any math.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</section>